RETRIEVED ARCHITECTURAL KNOWLEDGE
Contextual design options, not instructions, regulations or fixed geometry. Mandatory user requirements and exclusions and the architectural-plan-1 contract prevail over stored suggestions and parameter ranges; optional suggestions never override them. Combine relevant fragments; unknown site facts remain assumptions. A record whose id begins strategy- is a whole-house organising idea, not a template: adapt or decline it for this brief and site; its expression line says which composition and plan fields carry the idea and its limits line names what the contract cannot build. Stored knowledge is retrieval, not retraining.
{"id":"property-value-25m-50m","version":2,"category":"property-value","when":"US$25,000,000–50,000,000 estimated property-value label, used only as a subjective design-ambition reference. Property value is not a construction budget. Location, land value, market conditions, condition and other factors affect prices; this band establishes no market-price or cost claim.","title":"Design-ambition reference: Exceptional estate: integrated architecture","combinations":"Terra may combine, reinterpret or omit these subjective ideas with relevant regional, style, climate, entrance, roof, facade, material and room-planning knowledge. Amenities are optional inspirations, never mandatory. Do not constrain creative freedom through a fixed template, geometry, area, storey, room or amenity count.","placement":"Let the household brief, site, climate, privacy and architectural concept determine spatial relationships. Connect arrival, shared living and sheltered outdoor space where appropriate; separate guest, private and service movement when useful. No fixed floor areas, storey counts or room counts follow from the value label.","principles":"Explore an estate-wide composition with a strong main residence, private suites, connected living and sheltered entertaining. Compose a bespoke arrival, sophisticated roof relationships, deep facades and balanced materials. Pursue carefully resolved craftsmanship and spatial complexity only where useful. Guest facilities, a pool pavilion or wellness rooms may serve the brief without becoming an amenity checklist. These are flexible inspirations, not requirements or price-derived promises."}
{"id":"facade-materials","version":1,"category":"materials","when":"A residence needing coherent enclosure and material expression.","title":"Facade depth, roofs and material identity","combinations":"Coordinate a small material palette, roof edges, recessed entrances and shaded openings.","placement":"Relate facade rhythm to rooms and privacy; carry architectural intent around side and rear elevations.","principles":"Adapt material and roof recommendations to climate and style instead of prescribing a fixed house template."}
{"id":"strategy-waterfront-central-pavilion","version":1,"category":"composition","when":"A large residence on a water edge or strong view (estate or mansion) whose brief asks for a memorable arrival, one dominant gathering space and quiet private accommodation. Use when the water side can be stated or assumed. Decline for a compact plot, a site with no view axis, or a brief that wants a low, horizontal, evenly weighted house.","title":"Waterfront estate: dominant central pavilion with subordinate wings","combinations":"Roof: one hip or gable roofSpan over the pavilion with a deep overhang (about 1 m) over lower neighbours; one lower roofSpan per wing, overhang 0 where it abuts the taller wall. Facade: evenly spaced tall door bays on the water elevation under the terrace roof; narrow high-sill bays on wing faces for privacy. Limestone pavilion, timber wings. Shade comes from the terrace roof and eaves, not from more glass. Example: a 14 m terrace centred on an 8 m salon reaches 3 m past it, so a wing placed against the whole pavilion stands 3 m from the salon unless the terrace is attached.","placement":"Landward: arrival court and a roofed porch against the pavilion, with an entrance door as tall as the double-height hall allows. Seaward: living and dining open through tall doors onto a deep roofed terrace, then the pool on the same axis. Suites sit in wings joined to the pavilion flanks by a gap-0 gallery, so each is reached from the hall, never through living rooms. Each suite is a bedroom with its own joined bath space (a label is not a bathroom). Service and garage stay landward behind a wing; the primary suite may take the end of one wing.","principles":"Concept: one tall living pavilion faces the water; accommodation wings flank it and read as lesser. Hierarchy is made in section as well as plan: only the pavilion is primary, double-height or an added storey at least heightStep above the wings, under the simplest and highest roof; wings are lower with quieter roofs. Failure: equal boxes in a row, or a pavilion that is only labelled primary.","expression":"composition.volumes: pavilion masses {tier primary, roof: its roofSpan}; one volume per wing {secondary or subordinate, own roofSpan}; heightStep 1-1.5 m. Authored room heights carry the section (double-height rooms with nothing above). roofSpans only over contiguous same-height rooms that tile a rectangle. facades: kind doors with to: terrace; windows on exposed walls. entrance {portal, porch}: the porch is the arrival space, roofed, 0.1 m or more higher than the door. Wings: masses placed gap 0 against the pavilion; an adjoin constraint hall to wing. Declare the pavilion's porch, terrace and annexes attached:true so wings meet the salons.","limits":"Not expressible: a roof over an L or U union or over rooms of mixed height; valleys; recessed or projecting facade planes (depth comes only from placement, eaves and the porch); glazed-looking doors (door bays build as solid leaves); planting; a surveyed shore or setback. State the assumed water side, and that the site is flat, in limitations."}
{"id":"waterfront-estate","version":1,"category":"climate-site","when":"Sites with a stated water edge or coastal view.","title":"Waterfront privacy and site sequence","combinations":"Consider view terraces and pools; docks only with suitable water access, brief and permissions.","placement":"Protect the public shore edge and setbacks; place living towards views, private suites away from public routes, vehicle arrival landward when feasible.","principles":"Do not invent a surveyed shore, flood elevation or legal setback; keep site assumptions explicit."}
{"id":"living-view","version":1,"category":"programme","when":"Principal shared rooms with a stated outlook.","title":"Living room and view hierarchy","combinations":"Balance wide glazing with solid furniture walls.","placement":"Connect living to dining and a covered terrace with framed view openings.","principles":"Avoid exposed glass on every side."}
{"id":"legacy-recipe-65a14c7e-0559-45f0-adfe-589551ca499c","version":1,"category":"roof-outdoor","when":"Existing outdoor-living recipe; original compatible scales: mansion.","title":"Caribbean Hillside Dining Pavilion 02","combinations":"[{\"kind\":\"requires\",\"note\":\"within 10 m with direct service path\",\"target\":\"outdoor-kitchen\"},{\"kind\":\"requires\",\"note\":\"provide level, slip-resistant dining surface\",\"target\":\"patio\"},{\"kind\":\"prefers\",\"note\":\"covering table and circulation edge\",\"target\":\"pergola\"},{\"kind\":\"prefers\",\"note\":\"set above or beside pool with open ocean outlook\",\"target\":\"pool\"},{\"kind\":\"prefers\",\"note\":\"layer warm overhead and pathway lighting\",\"target\":\"light\"},{\"kind\":\"conflicts\",\"note\":\"screen service and vehicle noise from dining area\",\"target\":\"driveway\"}]","placement":"Place on the ocean-facing side of the house with a level retained platform. Orient the long table parallel to the primary view rather than blocking it. Use a ventilated pergola with adjustable shade for tropical sun and brief rain. Keep the kitchen service route direct, step-free, and visually discreet. Protect the uphill edge from gusts using louvers or layered tropical planting. Maintain an unobstructed circulation band around the table and toward the view edge.","principles":"Adapt approved guidance to the brief. Parameter options: [{\"key\":\"diningAreaWidth\",\"max\":14,\"min\":5,\"unit\":\"m\",\"value\":8},{\"key\":\"diningAreaDepth\",\"max\":10,\"min\":4,\"unit\":\"m\",\"value\":6},{\"key\":\"tableSeatCount\",\"max\":20,\"min\":8,\"unit\":\"count\",\"value\":12},{\"key\":\"pergolaCoverage\",\"max\":90,\"min\":35,\"unit\":\"%\",\"value\":65},{\"key\":\"viewEdgeClearance\",\"max\":5,\"min\":1.5,\"unit\":\"m\",\"value\":2.5},{\"key\":\"kitchenDistance\",\"max\":10,\"min\":2,\"unit\":\"m\",\"value\":5},{\"key\":\"ambientLightingLevel\",\"max\":150,\"min\":30,\"unit\":\"lux\",\"value\":80},{\"key\":\"windScreenType\",\"unit\":\"string\",\"value\":\"timber-louver\",\"options\":[\"none\",\"planting\",\"timber-louver\",\"glass-screen\"]}]."}
{"id":"tropical-envelope","version":1,"category":"envelope","when":"Hot humid coastal or island contexts.","title":"Caribbean climate and envelope","combinations":"Pair shaded glazing with sheltered outdoor living and selective screening.","placement":"Place openings for potential cross ventilation and shelter entrances from driving rain; orientation and wind are assumptions until supplied.","principles":"Prioritize shade, weather protection and maintainable coastal materials; request local hurricane and corrosion engineering."}
{"id":"covered-threshold","version":1,"category":"arrival","when":"Weather protection at the front door.","title":"Covered entrance threshold","combinations":"Choose wall returns or posts.","placement":"Connect approach slab to a recessed door under a supported canopy.","principles":"Avoid a door behind an uncut wall."}
{"id":"pool-edge-levels","version":1,"category":"amenity","when":"Use when the brief or chosen programme calls for pool deck and water datums.","title":"Pool deck and water datums","combinations":"Limestone basin-like enclosure and deck; water remains the dedicated rectangular surface primitive.","placement":"Keep water beneath basin rim and relate deck walking surface to safe-looking dry access.","principles":"No hydraulic freeboard or safety compliance is certified."}
{"id":"lesson-roof-over-void","version":1,"category":"construction","when":"Use when the brief or chosen programme calls for prevent covered double height void.","title":"Prevent covered double height void","combinations":"Preserve the established palette in targeted edits; use finish only on intended elements.","placement":"Coordinate upper floor plates around the intended open volume.","principles":"Do not close a promised void with an upper slab."}
{"id":"balinese-pavilion-links","version":1,"category":"tradition","when":"Use when the brief or chosen programme calls for balinese inspired pavilion relationships.","title":"Balinese inspired pavilion relationships","combinations":"Choose compatible ivory limestone timber or bronze from the scene palette; names do not imply specialist finishes.","placement":"Connect smaller roofed room shells through shaded outdoor circulation.","principles":"Do not use the open curved lounge builder as an enclosed bedroom."}
{"id":"estate-composition","version":1,"category":"composition","when":"Large private residences with substantial entertaining and service needs.","title":"Estate programme and hierarchy","combinations":"Consider guest accommodation and wellness only after a complete main residence.","placement":"Sequence arrival, entrance, living, shaded terrace and view; separate service access and private suites.","principles":"Give secondary volumes a programme and subordinate them to the residence."}
